What are User Defined Functions?

User Defined Functions (UDFs) are custom functions created by users to encapsulate reusable logic in SQL.

They:
  Accept inputs (parameters)
  Perform operations
  Return a value

Scalar UDF
        Returns a single value, Must specify RETURNS

        ex:   DELIMITER $$
              CREATE FUNCTION get_bonus(salary INT)                                usage:
              RETURNS INT                                                          SELECT name, get_bonus(salary)
              DETERMINISTIC                                                        FROM Employees;
              BEGIN
              RETURN salary * 0.10;
              END $$
              DELIMITER ;

Table-Valued UDF
        Returns a table of content

        Ex:  CREATE FUNCTION get_high_salary()
             RETURNS TABLE                                                         usage:
             AS                                                                    SELECT * FROM get_high_salary();
             RETURN (
                     SELECT * FROM Employees
                     WHERE salary > 50000
              );


Pre-Defined Functions:
  1. Aggregate Functions: Operate on multiple rows → return a single value.

            Function	    Description
            COUNT()	    Number of rows
            SUM()	        Total sum
            AVG()	       Average value
            MIN()	      Smallest value
            MAX()        Largest value

      
2.Scalar (Single-row) Functions: Operate on each row individually.
      Categories of Scalar Functions
        a) String Functions
           Function	        Description
            UPPER()	    Converts to uppercase
            LOWER()	    Converts to lowercase
            LENGTH()	    Returns string length
          SUBSTRING()	  Extracts part of string
            TRIM()	       Removes spaces


        b) Numeric Functions
              Function	          Description
              ROUND()	           Rounds value
              CEIL() / CEILING()	Rounds up
              FLOOR()	           Rounds down
              ABS()	            Absolute value
      
      
       c) Date Functions
              Function             	Description
              NOW()	            Current date & time
              CURDATE()	            Current date
              DATEDIFF()	    Difference between dates
              DATE_ADD()	          Add interval
      
      
       d) Conditional Functions
             SELECT name,
              CASE 
                WHEN salary > 50000 THEN 'High'
                ELSE 'Low'
              END AS salary_category
            FROM Employees;



            
                                                             Key Differences
                                         Type	                                Works On	Returns
                                      ---------------------------------------------------------------
                                        Aggregate	                        Multiple rows	Single value
                                        Scalar	                          Each row	One value per row
